1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,590
Speaker: In 1990, a small
mountain town asked America one

2
00:00:03,590 --> 00:00:06,280
question and refused to answer it.

3
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:11,030
The question was simple:
Who killed Laura Palmer?

4
00:00:12,259 --> 00:00:16,630
The show was Twin Peaks, and for a while,
that single unanswered question was

5
00:00:16,630 --> 00:00:19,900
strong enough to make people rearrange
their week around a television schedule.

6
00:00:21,369 --> 00:00:24,170
The question this video asks
is not which dramas from the

7
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:26,140
'90s people remember fondly.

8
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:29,490
Nostalgia explains why
people rewatch a show.

9
00:00:30,109 --> 00:00:34,230
It does not explain why people showed
up for it in the first place, week

10
00:00:34,230 --> 00:00:38,609
after week, back before streaming made
a fixed schedule almost meaningless.

11
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:44,540
The real question is what these shows were
actually doing mechanically that turned

12
00:00:44,549 --> 00:00:48,089
watching television into an appointment
instead of a background activity.

13
00:00:49,489 --> 00:00:54,790
Three answers in order: how they used
ensembles, how they used cliffhangers,

14
00:00:55,129 --> 00:00:56,749
and how they earned their stakes.

15
00:00:57,919 --> 00:01:02,040
And one of those answers comes with a
warning because the same network that

16
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:07,449
helped perfect the technique also broke
it on purpose in someone else's show.

17
00:01:08,309 --> 00:01:09,809
Start with the ensemble.

18
00:01:10,349 --> 00:01:16,759
When ER premiered on NBC in September
of 1994, it did not center on one lead

19
00:01:16,769 --> 00:01:18,650
the way most dramas before it had.

20
00:01:19,689 --> 00:01:23,949
It followed an emergency room full of
doctors and nurses, cutting between

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,109
overlapping crises in something
close to real time so that a quiet

22
00:01:28,139 --> 00:01:32,629
character beat in one hallway sat
right next to a life-or-death decision

23
00:01:32,639 --> 00:01:38,908
in the next room The two-hour pilot
alone introduced a full roster: Dr.

24
00:01:38,908 --> 00:01:40,388
Mark Greene, Dr.

25
00:01:40,388 --> 00:01:41,917
Doug Ross, Dr.

26
00:01:41,917 --> 00:01:43,488
Susan Lewis, Dr.

27
00:01:43,488 --> 00:01:47,608
John Carter, and Nurse Carol
Hathaway, none of them positioned

28
00:01:47,608 --> 00:01:49,058
as more important than the others.

29
00:01:50,418 --> 00:01:53,988
The show became the top-rated program
on American television through the mid

30
00:01:53,988 --> 00:01:58,618
to late '90s, regularly pulling more
than thirty million viewers, and a

31
00:01:58,618 --> 00:02:00,718
rotating ensemble was central to why.

32
00:02:01,718 --> 00:02:04,377
No single actor's schedule or
storyline could stall the whole

33
00:02:04,378 --> 00:02:08,127
machine because the show was never
built around just one person.

34
00:02:09,167 --> 00:02:13,848
When one doctor's arc slowed down, three
others were already mid-crisis, and the

35
00:02:13,848 --> 00:02:17,547
show's fast, overlapping camera style
made that constant motion feel less

36
00:02:17,548 --> 00:02:21,628
like a writing choice and more like the
actual pace of a real emergency room.

37
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:25,567
The audience was never waiting on one
character to get interesting again.

38
00:02:26,407 --> 00:02:29,098
That structure did something
else too, something easy to miss.

39
00:02:29,517 --> 00:02:32,017
It meant a viewer could form
a favorite without the show

40
00:02:32,048 --> 00:02:33,147
depending on that favorite.

41
00:02:34,618 --> 00:02:38,288
Twenty different people could watch
the same episode of ER and each walk

42
00:02:38,288 --> 00:02:42,027
away invested in a different doctor,
which multiplied the reasons to come

43
00:02:42,028 --> 00:02:45,608
back next week instead of concentrating
all of it onto one performance

44
00:02:45,608 --> 00:02:47,157
that might eventually go stale.

45
00:02:47,547 --> 00:02:50,818
It also meant the show could survive
a cast departure that would have sunk

46
00:02:50,818 --> 00:02:54,787
a single-lead drama outright since a
rotating ensemble was designed from

47
00:02:54,788 --> 00:02:59,137
the beginning to absorb exactly that
kind of change without the audience

48
00:02:59,138 --> 00:03:00,737
losing its reason to keep watching.

49
00:03:02,097 --> 00:03:05,267
Now the cliffhanger and
the show that did it best.

50
00:03:06,638 --> 00:03:12,127
The X-Files, which premiered on Fox
in September of 1993, ran two kinds

51
00:03:12,127 --> 00:03:17,417
of episodes side by side: freestanding
stories, often called Monster of the Week,

52
00:03:17,897 --> 00:03:22,567
built around FBI agents Mulder and Scully
investigating a single strange case,

53
00:03:22,907 --> 00:03:27,357
and a connected mythology arc about a
government conspiracy that the two of them

54
00:03:27,357 --> 00:03:30,177
kept almost but never fully uncovering.

55
00:03:31,297 --> 00:03:35,357
The mythology episodes were rationed
deliberately rather than run constantly,

56
00:03:35,877 --> 00:03:39,407
and the show became known for ending
entire seasons on some of the biggest

57
00:03:40,528 --> 00:03:41,727
cliffhangers television had produced.

58
00:03:42,647 --> 00:03:46,678
One widely cited example leaving
both lead characters in mortal danger

59
00:03:46,928 --> 00:03:50,497
with no resolution offered until
the next season actually began.

60
00:03:51,387 --> 00:03:53,717
That rationing was the
real mechanism at work.

61
00:03:54,187 --> 00:03:57,798
A cliffhanger only functions as
appointment television if it is rare

62
00:03:57,798 --> 00:04:01,517
enough that the audience cannot predict
when the next one is coming, so they

63
00:04:01,517 --> 00:04:05,077
simply have to keep watching every
single week in case this is the one.

64
00:04:06,547 --> 00:04:13,050
Here is the warning In 1990, ABC had
arguably the biggest cliffhanger engine on

65
00:04:13,050 --> 00:04:15,290
television, and they broke it on purpose.

66
00:04:16,730 --> 00:04:20,339
Twin Peaks had built its entire first
season around the open question of who

67
00:04:20,339 --> 00:04:25,119
killed Laura Palmer, and creator David
Lynch never intended to answer it at all.

68
00:04:25,909 --> 00:04:29,589
He wanted the mystery itself
to be the engine permanently,

69
00:04:30,039 --> 00:04:33,329
driving the show indefinitely
without ever cashing itself in.

70
00:04:33,750 --> 00:04:37,799
Network executives disagreed, worried
that an unanswered question could

71
00:04:37,799 --> 00:04:41,739
not sustain a series forever, and
pressured the writers to reveal the

72
00:04:41,739 --> 00:04:43,629
killer partway through season two.

73
00:04:44,219 --> 00:04:47,759
The reveal aired in November of
1990 in the seventh episode of that

74
00:04:47,759 --> 00:04:52,714
season Viewership began dropping
almost immediately afterward.

75
00:04:53,183 --> 00:04:55,763
The mystery, it turned out,
was not one ingredient in the

76
00:04:55,763 --> 00:04:57,203
show's appeal among several.

77
00:04:57,474 --> 00:04:58,383
It was load-bearing.

78
00:04:59,383 --> 00:05:03,183
Once it resolved, a meaningful share
of the audience simply no longer had

79
00:05:03,183 --> 00:05:06,973
a reason to show up on a given week,
and the show was canceled not long

80
00:05:06,983 --> 00:05:10,803
after on a cliffhanger nobody stuck
around long enough to see resolved.

81
00:05:11,753 --> 00:05:15,083
That is the part modern television
forgot in the other direction.

82
00:05:15,803 --> 00:05:19,873
The difference between a cliffhanger
you ration carefully and a mystery

83
00:05:19,873 --> 00:05:23,694
you cash in too early because someone
outside the writers' room got nervous.

84
00:05:25,053 --> 00:05:28,914
Networks and streamers now regularly
answer a show's central question

85
00:05:28,933 --> 00:05:33,443
inside its very first season, treating
a serialized mystery as a retention

86
00:05:33,443 --> 00:05:35,784
risk if it runs too long unresolved.

87
00:05:37,173 --> 00:05:40,414
Twin Peaks is the proof that the
opposite failure mode is just as

88
00:05:40,414 --> 00:05:42,123
real and arguably more permanent.

89
00:05:43,243 --> 00:05:46,783
Answer the question too soon on
someone else's schedule instead of

90
00:05:46,783 --> 00:05:50,103
the story's own, and you can kill
the exact thing that was pulling

91
00:05:50,103 --> 00:05:51,483
people back in the first place.

92
00:05:52,563 --> 00:05:56,063
The third mechanic is the hardest
one to fake: stakes that feel

93
00:05:56,093 --> 00:05:58,143
earned, not staged for shock.

94
00:05:59,143 --> 00:06:04,344
NYPD Blue, created by Steven Bochco
and David Milch and built in part

95
00:06:04,344 --> 00:06:08,584
from Milch's own experience with his
father's death, spent years establishing

96
00:06:08,584 --> 00:06:13,013
Detective Bobby Simone as a steady,
reliable presence on the show before

97
00:06:13,014 --> 00:06:15,283
ever writing him toward a fatal illness.

98
00:06:15,853 --> 00:06:19,423
Actor Jimmy Smits had appeared in roughly
ninety episodes of the series by the

99
00:06:19,423 --> 00:06:23,943
time his character's death aired in the
fifth episode of season six, and the show

100
00:06:23,943 --> 00:06:27,933
let that death play out slowly across
several episodes rather than resolving

101
00:06:27,933 --> 00:06:29,703
it inside a single dramatic hour.

102
00:06:31,073 --> 00:06:34,313
The series had spent years training
its audience to believe Bobby Simone

103
00:06:34,313 --> 00:06:39,364
was safe, dependable, the character
nothing serious ever happened to.

104
00:06:40,753 --> 00:06:45,343
Killing him meant nobody on that show
was safe again, and every dramatic

105
00:06:45,343 --> 00:06:49,323
moment that followed carried real
weight because the audience had already

106
00:06:49,323 --> 00:06:53,214
watched the show prove at real cost
that it meant what it was threatening.

107
00:06:54,413 --> 00:06:57,523
That is the piece that is hardest
to manufacture on a compressed

108
00:06:57,523 --> 00:07:01,003
schedule because it requires
patience most current writers'

109
00:07:01,003 --> 00:07:02,773
rooms are simply not given anymore.

110
00:07:03,573 --> 00:07:07,613
A death like that cannot be earned
inside a six-episode season with a

111
00:07:07,614 --> 00:07:09,484
cast the audience barely knows yet.

112
00:07:10,194 --> 00:07:14,074
It requires years of ordinary,
unremarkable competence from a character

113
00:07:14,074 --> 00:07:18,063
first so that losing them actually
costs the audience something real

114
00:07:18,063 --> 00:07:20,133
instead of registering as a plot twist.

115
00:07:20,584 --> 00:07:24,363
A show has to be willing to spend
genuine time building a person up

116
00:07:24,683 --> 00:07:28,423
before it can afford to take them
away and have the loss actually land.

117
00:07:29,903 --> 00:07:33,263
Put the three mechanics together
and a pattern shows up clearly.

118
00:07:33,873 --> 00:07:37,644
Ensembles that spread the risk across
many characters instead of resting

119
00:07:37,694 --> 00:07:42,120
everything on one Cliffhangers
rationed carefully enough that

120
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:45,039
the audience could never predict
exactly when the next one was coming.

121
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:48,719
Stakes built slowly enough that
a loss actually registered as

122
00:07:48,719 --> 00:07:50,199
a loss rather than a headline.

123
00:07:50,729 --> 00:07:54,359
None of these are stylistic quirks
left over from a bygone decade.

124
00:07:54,989 --> 00:07:58,659
They are structural decisions about
pacing and patience, and every one

125
00:07:58,659 --> 00:08:02,599
of them requires a show and a network
standing behind it, willing to let

126
00:08:02,599 --> 00:08:04,440
a story take its time to pay off.

127
00:08:05,239 --> 00:08:09,130
Twin Peaks demonstrates exactly what
happens when that patience gets overridden

128
00:08:09,130 --> 00:08:10,869
from the outside for a ratings sweep.

129
00:08:10,879 --> 00:08:18,509
ER and NYPD Blue show what patience
built instead when nobody interfered

130
00:08:18,509 --> 00:08:22,099
with it and what it looked like
when a network simply let a writer's

131
00:08:22,099 --> 00:08:23,879
room finish what it had started.

132
00:08:25,149 --> 00:08:28,419
Modern television did not misplace
these mechanics by accident.

133
00:08:28,939 --> 00:08:33,259
It largely traded them away on purpose,
swapping a slow-built ensemble and

134
00:08:33,259 --> 00:08:37,069
a rationed cliffhanger for a pilot
engineered to hook an algorithm in

135
00:08:37,069 --> 00:08:41,250
its first ten minutes rather than an
audience across several patient seasons.

136
00:08:42,079 --> 00:08:45,330
The dramas that made appointment
television work in the '90s were not

137
00:08:45,330 --> 00:08:46,850
smarter than what came after them.

138
00:08:47,649 --> 00:08:51,110
They were simply allowed to be
patient, and they were given enough

139
00:08:51,110 --> 00:08:54,170
time on the air to prove that the
wait had actually been worth it
